#!/usr/bin/python3
"""
workload-vm-resolve: one filtered VM's synthesising DNS responder.

Usage: workload-vm-resolve <name>   (socket-activated; never binds its own)

WHAT IT IS

The guest's only nameserver. Every A/AAAA, for any name, is answered with this
workload's inspector address, so the guest's connect() lands on the listener its
80 and 443 are redirected to anyway. Every other query type is NODATA.

NOTHING IS FORWARDED. There is no upstream socket in this program -- no
connect(), no getaddrinfo(), no resolver library call anywhere in it. That is
what makes DNS exfiltration ABSENT rather than filtered: a guest cannot tunnel
data out through a nameserver that never speaks to anything. It is also the
first property to check if anyone ever "adds a fallback for names we don't
serve"; there are no names we don't serve.

WHY NOT DNSMASQ

This is the obvious question and it has a real answer, because dnsmasq does
exactly this feature in about eight lines of config:

    port=53 / listen-address=<responder addr> / bind-interfaces
    no-resolv / no-poll
    address=/#/<inspector addr>        # synthesise every name
    host-record=<name>,<addr>          # the allow-by-name static map

It is battle-tested where this is not, it handles EDNS0, TCP framing,
truncation and 0x20 randomisation for free, and running one per workload is the
same shape as the inspector beside it. The reason it is not used here is
narrower than "not invented here":

  * THE PROPERTY IS NEGATIVE. The inspector's job is positive -- allow these
    hosts -- so a misconfiguration is visible and tends toward refusing
    traffic. This
    program's job is "never speaks upstream", and dnsmasq's DEFAULT is to
    forward. The security property would then be carried by the ABSENCE of
    configuration: a /etc/dnsmasq.d drop-in, a changed packaging default, or an
    operator adding `server=` for a reasonable-looking purpose reopens the
    exfiltration channel silently, and nothing fails. Here it is a property of
    the source text, and tests/test_vm_resolve.py asserts it by parsing every
    file the responder is made of and rejecting any call that could reach a
    nameserver.
  * PORT 53 AS A NON-ROOT USER. dnsmasq binds its own socket, so running it as
    _wl-<name> needs CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE on a per-workload daemon parsing
    guest-controlled input, or a high port and another redirect rule. The
    inherited fd avoids both.
  * ADR 006 ALREADY DELETED IT. dnsmasq went with the managed bridge; passt
    serves the guest DHCP. Re-adding a per-workload dnsmasq is a reversal that
    belongs in an ADR, not in an implementation choice.

The cost of that decision is a wire parser of our own (lib/dns_wire.py), and it
is paid down where it can be: build_answer is a pure bytes -> bytes function
with no I/O behind it, so TestFuzz feeds it garbage in bulk. It has already found one defect class
(a name ending exactly at the message boundary), which is pinned as its own
test.

WHY A SOCKET UNIT, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT BORROW

One reason only: port 53 is privileged and this runs as _wl-<name>. It borrows
nothing else from the inspector's socket unit (§7.7) -- no address-add helper,
no FreeBind= question, no teardown that makes an address mean a running
process. The kernel treats all of 127/8 as local on `lo`, so the responder's
address needs no assignment and adding one would be a no-op that later reads as
load-bearing.

RESPONSE CODES: NODATA, NEVER REFUSED

HTTPS/SVCB, TXT, PTR, MX, SRV and everything else get NOERROR with an empty
answer section. Not REFUSED: the guest's resolver list has exactly one entry, so
there is no second nameserver for a fallback to reach. REFUSED reads to a stub
as "this server is broken, try another", and with nothing to try it costs a full
retry schedule per lookup -- which presents as "DNS is slow in this guest" and
points at everything except this file.

EDNS0

A query carrying OPT gets a well-formed answer WITHOUT an OPT of our own. That
is a decision, not an oversight: systemd-resolved (the actual client on Fedora
Cloud) reads a missing OPT as no EDNS0 support and downgrades once, which costs
one round trip on the first query of a connection and nothing after. The shape
to avoid is a malformed or truncated OPT echoed back, which is what makes a stub
mark a server bad -- with nowhere to fall back to.

STATED LIMIT: no SOA is emitted with a NODATA answer, so a stub cannot
negative-cache one. Repeat MX/TXT lookups therefore reach us every time. They
are answered from memory with no I/O; the cost is a syscall, not a timeout.
"""
import signal
import sys

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True

from dns_wire import log
from nft_elements import vm_resolve_status_path
from resolve_policy import load_policy
from resolve_server import Counters, inherited_listening_sockets, serve
from customs.sd_listen import NotSocketActivated
from vm_network_config import vm_resolve_policy_path


def main(argv):
    if len(argv) != 2:
        print(f"usage: {argv[0]} <name>", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    name = argv[1]
    sockets = inherited_listening_sockets()
    if not sockets:
        raise NotSocketActivated("LISTEN_FDS=0: no sockets were passed")
    policy = load_policy(vm_resolve_policy_path(name))
    log(f"  responder for {name}: {len(sockets)} socket(s), "
        f"{len(policy.static)} static name(s), "
        f"{len(policy.hosts)} host pattern(s), "
        f"{len(policy.policy)} policy pattern(s), ttl {policy.ttl}s")
    counters = Counters()
    stopping = []
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_a: stopping.append(True))
    serve(sockets, policy, counters, vm_resolve_status_path(name),
          stop=lambda: bool(stopping))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(0)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        print(f"  ERROR: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
